## 🛠️ Network Discovery & Reconnaissance ##

These tools were used to identify the target and map the available entry points.

netdiscover: Used for active/passive ARP reconnaissance to find the target's IP address on the local subnet.

nmap (Network Mapper): The primary tool for port scanning, service version detection (-sV), and running default vulnerability scripts (-sC).

##🧨 Exploitation & Initial Access##

These tools allowed you to bridge the gap between the web interface and the server's operating system.

Web Browser (Firefox/Chrome): Used to manually enumerate hidden directories (/dev_shell.php, /lat_memo.html) discovered by Nmap.

bash: Utilized as the primary payload language to establish a TCP-based reverse shell (/dev/tcp/...).

netcat (nc): Used on your Kali machine to set up a listener (-lvnp) to catch the incoming connection from the target.

##📈 Post-Exploitation & Enumeration##

Once initial access was gained, these built-in Linux utilities were used to find sensitive data and move laterally.

find: Used to search the entire filesystem for sensitive files containing the string "password."

ls: Used with flags like -laR to recursively list all files, including hidden ones, in the home directories of bob and elliot.

cat: Used to read the contents of harvested files like .old_passwordfile.html and theadminisdumb.txt.

ssh: Used to pivot from the unstable web shell to a secure, encrypted interactive session on the non-standard port 25468.

##🔑 Privilege Escalation & Cryptography##

These tools were used to crack security layers and achieve administrative (root) authority.

gpg (GNU Privacy Guard): Specifically used to decrypt the login.txt.gpg file using a discovered passphrase to reveal the administrator's password.

su (Substitute User): Used to switch between accounts (seb, jc, bob) as you moved through the lateral movement phase.

sudo: Used with the -i flag to elevate your session to a root shell once the administrative password was recovered.

id: The definitive tool used to verify that your effective user ID had reached 0 (root).

##🧹 Housekeeping & Anti-Forensics##

These tools were used to clean up the environment and hide the footprints of the attack.

history: Used with the -c flag to wipe the command buffer of the active shell.

rm: Used to delete physical artifacts like .bash_history and any uploaded shell scripts.

truncate: Used to zero out system and web logs (auth.log, access.log, error.log) to hide the intrusion without crashing system services.
